Eludite @VERSION@ for Linux (@ARCH@)
====================================

Run it from this folder:

    ./eludite --folder ~/src/my-project        (or --solution path/to/App.slnx)

Nothing here needs installing, and Eludite needs no environment variables: the shell finds its browser engine
(eludite-chromium) beside itself, and the engine finds CEF in cef/ beside itself. Eludite makes no network call at
startup; the engine starts when the Web Browser window (View > Other Windows > Web Browser) or a browser tool first
needs it.

To add Eludite to your desktop's menus, put this folder where it will stay (for example ~/.local/opt/eludite), then:

    sed "s|^Exec=eludite|Exec=$PWD/eludite|; s|^TryExec=eludite|TryExec=$PWD/eludite|" eludite.desktop \
      > ~/.local/share/applications/eludite.desktop
    cp -r icons/hicolor ~/.local/share/icons/

The sandbox
-----------

The embedded browser runs web pages in Chromium's sandbox. On Linux that needs one of:

1. Unprivileged user namespaces, which most distributions allow (nothing to do). Debian-style kernels can turn them
   off with kernel.unprivileged_userns_clone=0; Ubuntu 23.10 and later restrict them through AppArmor unless a
   profile permits them for eludite-chromium.
2. Otherwise, Chromium's setuid helper, installed once by root:

       sudo chown root:root cef/chrome-sandbox && sudo chmod 4755 cef/chrome-sandbox

   Chromium uses a helper outside the engine's own folder only when eludite-chromium belongs to the user running
   it, as it does when you unpack this archive yourself. If root unpacked it (for example into /opt), install the
   helper beside the engine instead: copy cef/chrome-sandbox to ./chrome-sandbox, then run the two commands on it.

With neither (or when Eludite runs as root, which Chromium never sandboxes), the engine refuses to start and the Web
Browser window says why. Its dialog offers "Run without the sandbox for this workspace", which stores
browser.allowNoSandbox in the workspace's .eludite/settings.json; Tools > Options > Web Browser turns it off again.
While the engine runs without the sandbox, the window shows a strip saying so.

Files
-----

    eludite                  the IDE (it never loads CEF)
    eludite-chromium         the browser engine, CEF's browser process
    cef/                     CEF 154 (Chromium 154) runtime files and the sandbox helper
    eludite.desktop, icons/  the desktop launcher and icons
    LICENSE                  GNU General Public License, version 3 (Eludite is GPL-3.0-or-later)
    cef/LICENSE.txt          CEF's license (BSD-3-Clause)
    cef/CREDITS.html         Chromium's third-party licenses
    THIRD-PARTY-CRATES.txt   the Rust crates linked into the two executables, with their licenses

H.264 and AAC media do not play (CEF's build without proprietary codecs).
